      "title": "Megan Grey's claims: Google manipulates Search for ad revenue",
      "summary": "Megan Grey, former DuckDuckGo vice-president, admits possibly misinterpreting evidence but maintains Google manipulates Search to maximise ad revenue, despite Google's denial via Danny Sullivan that ad systems affect organic results."
    },
    {
      "title": "Google's official response: Ad keyword matching vs organic results",
      "summary": "Google's Danny Sullivan states ad keyword matching is a long-standing process for connecting people to relevant ads, while a separate process matches organic results to queries using meaning matching systems that handle spelling mistakes and synonyms."
    },
    {
      "title": "Google denies pushing users towards monetisable subjects",
      "summary": "While some queries result in more ads varying by request due to keyword matching, Google denies pushing users towards the most monetisable subjects."
    },
    {
      "title": "Search quality improved due to data volume increase from 2013 to 2023",
      "summary": "Data creation increased from around 9 ZB (nine trillion gigabytes) per year in 2013 to 120 ZB in 2023, shifting from static websites to live information nested in services and apps, making the assumption that search was worse due to monetisation a stretch."
    },
    {
      "title": "Google Search index size and ranking processes",
      "summary": "Google's central index is over 100 million gigabytes; Search uses quick processes to decide query meaning, looks beyond typed words, uses machine learning to rank results by quality and usability, and may refine results using user location and web history if allowed."
    },
    {
      "title": "Google quality testing and semantic matching criticism",
      "summary": "Google uses independent search quality raters and ran almost 900,000 quality tests in 2022 to ensure results are justifiably related; critics like Megan Grey contend Google uses semantic keyword matching to make searches less precise on purpose to widen content pools and increase ad auctions."
    }
  ]
}

